==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ML.|thaumatūrgus}}, from {{der|en|grc|θαυματουργός}}, from {{suf|grc|θαῦμα|alt1=θαύματο-|t1=marvel|-ουργός|t2=worker, maker|nocat=1}}. {{doublet|en|thaumaturgus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθɔːməˌtɜː(ɹ)d͡ʒ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-thaumaturge.wav|Audio (Berkshire, UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[performer]] of [[thaumaturgy]]; a performer of [[miracle]]s; a [[magician]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|title=Simon Magus|author=George Robert Stow Mead|authorlink=G. R. S. Mead|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/12892/12892-h/12892-h.htm|passage=Though the apostles are naturally shown as rejecting with indignation the pecuniary offer of the '''thaumaturge''', they display no hate for his personality, whereas the fathers depict him as the vilest of impostors and charlatans and hold him up to universal execration.}}

